  "title": "# Build: tab-group-assets (FABLE, shared asset type + framework-wide rule = foundation work) ## Wha…",
  "asked": "# Build: tab-group-assets (FABLE, shared asset type + framework-wide rule = foundation work) ## What Brett said (verbatim, Nov 4 2026, voice) Looking at UI Review's row (Design packs, Whole apps, Screens and pages, Settings interfaces, Dialogs, Menus), which switches the list = a tab group: \"If it's a tab group, why can't I get to the tab group settings by right-clicking on it?\" ... \"Needs to be. Yeah, all tab groups are supposed to be assets. With a settings interface, that's also an asset.\" Book to a free named agent. ## Do - **`tab-group@1` asset type**: if main-area-tabs or another build already started one, extend it rather than making a second. Schema in asset-types.json, Android kernel model/validator, web reference engine, conformance fixtures, PORTING.md. Settings: style (underline / pills / segmented / chips / boxed), position (top / left / right / bottom), display (icons + text / text / icons), overflow (scroll / wrap / more menu / dropdown), size/density, sticky, collapsible, closable/reorderable (where the host allows), badge counts on/off, remember last tab. Tokens-only styling. Ship about 6 starter assets and add a default to defaults.json. - **Every tab group gets a settings interface that is itself an asset**: it renders through `settings-interface@1` (from settings-interface-2; if that hasn't landed, build on its contract and coordinate via THREADS). Right-click / long-press any tab group: Settings / Change tab style (asset picker) / Move. Same plumbing as toolbars, banners and file menus. - **Migrate every hard-coded tab row** in the console, Molly, AmberX web and axHub web onto the shared tab-group component (UI Review's category row first, then Build manager, Plan Studio, Settings, Terminal, etc). List every one you converted in the report. - **Make it a rule**: add to _claude/rules/RULES.md and PORTING.md that all tab groups are tab-group assets with an asset settings interface. Add a CI check (like check-toolbars) that fails on new hard-coded tab rows, with a ratchet count for any left over. - Console controls so Amber can change a tab group's settings by voice. - Tests, UI Review shots (non-blocking), PR, auto-land when green, redeploy affected apps. Report in _claude/builds/reports/. Commits clean. ## ADDENDUM 1 (Brett, voice, Nov 4): same rule for ALL UI chrome \"Right now, I want to make sure that navigation menus, toolbars, banners, are all asset types, and they all have a settings interface, which is an asset, and we can get to their settings by right-clicking all of them.\" - Audit **navigation menus, toolbars, banners and tab groups** across the console, Molly, AmberX web and axHub web. For each one: (1) is it a real asset type (nav-menu / toolbar / banner / tab-group), (2) does its settings screen render through a `settings-interface@1` asset, (3) does right-click / long-press open Settings / Change (asset picker) / Move. - Fix every gap. If nav menus aren't an asset type yet (molly-nav-submenus may be doing that), coordinate via THREADS and either finish it here or take only the parts that build doesn't. - One shared 'chrome context menu' plus a settings-host so all four behave the same. - The rule plus a CI check covers all four, not just tab groups. - The report has a table: every instance, its type, and asset / settings-interface / right-click status before and after. ## ADDENDUM 2 (Brett, voice, Nov 4): default + per-app settings interfaces \"Every asset type that has the settings interface, there's going to be a default settings interface for all, used by default. And then there will be another one that will make a customized one for every app. And by default, the custom one will be selected.\" - For every asset type with a settings interface (nav-menu, toolbar, banner, tab-group, jump-menu, plus any other that has one): ship **one shared default** `settings-interface@1` asset (framework/assets). - **Each app gets its own custom copy** of it, created automatically from the default (at app ini…",
